WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.580
When a young neuron loses its survival signal, does it simply run down?

2
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.460
Or does it do something?

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.480
Picture three wells of young rat sympathetic neurons.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.140
These neurons depend on nerve growth factor, NGF.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.920
The first well keeps its NGF.

6
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:21.920
Those neurons live.

7
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:24.903
The second well loses its NGF.

8
00:00:25.923 --> 00:00:28.263
Most of those neurons die within a day or two.

9
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:31.413
The third well loses its NGF too.

10
00:00:32.253 --> 00:00:34.773
It also gets a drug that stops protein synthesis.

11
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:37.237
What will that drug do?

12
00:00:38.057 --> 00:00:41.157
Will these neurons die sooner, later, or on the same schedule?

13
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.210
Later. The death is delayed.

14
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.260
In 1988, Johnson's laboratory reported this result.

15
00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:01.890
Inhibitors of RNA synthesis, or of protein synthesis, kept deprived neurons alive.

16
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.170
Why is that strange?

17
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.730
A cell starved of what it needs should simply run down.

18
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:13.033
Stopping protein synthesis should, if anything, make it run down faster.

19
00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:17.263
Instead, the neuron has to make something in order to die.

20
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.167
Its death is something the cell does.

21
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:21.967
It is a program.

22
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.730
Pathologists describe two broad ways that cells die.

23
00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:27.830
The first is necrosis.

24
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.260
In necrosis, after trauma or loss of blood supply, the cell swells.

25
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.160
Its membranes fail.

26
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:40.133
Its contents spill out, and the tissue around it becomes inflamed.

27
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:42.673
The second is apoptosis.

28
00:01:43.573 --> 00:01:45.213
In apoptosis, the cell shrinks.

29
00:01:46.073 --> 00:01:48.393
Its chromatin condenses into dense clumps.

30
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:53.133
It breaks into membrane-wrapped pieces, and other cells swallow them.

31
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:56.847
A nucleus condensed this way is called pyknotic.

32
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.490
Early studies of neuron death in embryos counted these pyknotic nuclei.

33
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:05.793
The neuron's program raises three puzzles.

34
00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:10.247
First, what must a deprived neuron make, in order to die?

35
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.490
Second, which proteins carry out the death?

36
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:17.963
Third, what keeps those proteins off while NGF is present?

37
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:20.613
So hold on to one fact.

38
00:02:21.453 --> 00:02:25.313
A deprived young sympathetic neuron in culture does not just fade.

39
00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:28.833
It runs a program that needs new RNA and protein.
